diagnosis_text:
The root cause of PC1's intermittent-feeling internet slowness is that the campus DNS server (srv1, IP 10.5.0.4) is configured with `recursion no;` in its BIND9 `/etc/bind/named.conf`, making it an authoritative-only resolver that can answer queries for the internal zone `jeremysitlab.com` but immediately returns REFUSED to every external domain query (confirmed with `dig google.com @10.5.0.4` → `status: REFUSED, recursion requested but not available`).

PC1's `/etc/resolv.conf` has `nameserver 10.5.0.4` as its sole DNS server. Each time a user tries to open any website not already in the browser's or OS's DNS cache, the query is sent to srv1, receives an immediate REFUSED, and the name resolution fails. Pages backed by cached DNS entries load normally, while those requiring fresh DNS lookups hang until the OS resolver times out and the browser shows an error or retries. This creates the "on and off" pattern the user describes — working for recently visited or pre-cached sites, failing or stalling for new ones — producing the perceived picture of "slow/laggy internet."

The IP data plane is completely healthy: pings from PC1 to ispa/ispb and to srv1 itself show zero packet loss and sub-3 ms latency. The WAN DHCP leases on r1 are renewing cleanly every ~300 seconds. There is no traffic shaping, no OSPF instability, and no NAT misconfiguration.

The fix is to enable recursion on srv1 and configure upstream forwarders. Concretely: in `/etc/bind/named.conf`, change `recursion no;` to `recursion yes;` and add `forwarders { 203.0.113.1; };` (ispa's LAN-facing IP) so that srv1 can forward unresolvable queries to the simulated internet gateway. Then reload BIND9 with `rndc reload`.
=== END ===